{#
  The server's Python files live in files/, not templates/: validate/k8s_manifests.py renders
  every *.j2 under templates/ and parses it as YAML, and a Python script is not a manifest.
  Carrying them via lookup('file') also means this rendered ConfigMap changes whenever any of
  them does, so the central rollout-restart in roles/k8s/manifests picks the change up — no
  checksum/ pod annotation of its own is needed.

  Which files those are is artifacts_modules in defaults/main.yml, looped over below. A new
  module is added THERE and nowhere else: the two crash-loops recorded next were both caused
  by hand-editing keys into this document, and a loop has no place to hand-edit.

  Two rules for comments in this file, each learned by crash-looping the pod.

  ONE: never write a comment closing tag inside a comment. Quoting it does not escape it —
  Jinja ends the comment at the first one it sees, and every remaining line of prose renders
  into the document as YAML. That is why this block describes the tags in words instead of
  showing them.

  TWO: keep comments at column 0, above the document, never indented between two keys. An
  indented comment above known_services.json pushed that key inside the script's block
  scalar, and the pod crash-looped on `SyntaxError: invalid syntax` with YAML appended to the
  Python. Rendering strips the comment but keeps the two spaces that preceded it, and
  trim_blocks removes the newline after its closing tag, so those two spaces join the next
  line's two and the key lands at FOUR spaces: inside the block rather than beside it.
  Chaining keys after an embedded file is fine on its own — home-assistant's ConfigMap does
  it five times over.

  Why the local validator passed it: an absorbed key still parses as VALID YAML. It is one
  key whose value carries some extra lines, so a guard that only checks "does this parse"
  cannot see it — the flags match Ansible's (scripts/lib/render_guard.py), the check simply is
  not looking for this. test_artifacts_configmap.py asserts every key survives and that each
  embedded module is parseable Python, which is what actually pins it.

  The names the indexer tags documents with are built from the live inventory rather than
  hand-listed, so a service added to either host starts being recognised on the next deploy.
  BOTH hosts: daniel-box alone would miss the Pi's Docker services, and artifacts discussing
  wg-easy or glances are exactly the ones a daniel-box-only list cannot tag. Platform
  components (Longhorn, k3s) carry no containers_list entry at all, and retired names are
  added on top — the oldest artifacts are about kopia, portainer and watchtower, which no
  inventory lists any more, and those are the documents that most need a way in.
#}
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: artifacts-server
  namespace: {{ k8s_namespace }}
data:
  known_services.json: |
    {{ (
         (containers_list | map(attribute='name') | list)
         + (hostvars['daniel-pi'].containers_list | default([]) | map(attribute='name') | list)
         + artifacts_platform_services
         + artifacts_retired_services
       ) | unique | sort | tojson }}
{% for module in artifacts_modules %}
  {{ module }}: |
{{ lookup('file', playbook_dir + '/roles/k8s/artifacts/files/' + module) | indent(4, true) }}
{% endfor %}
